@anon.879c592214345ca999bafaa324406990.815 = private unnamed_addr constant [803 x i8] c"## What it does\0AChecks for usage of `datetime.datetime.today()`.\0A\0A## Why is this bad?\0A`datetime` objects are \22naive\22 by default, in that they do not include\0Atimezone information. \22Naive\22 objects are easy to understand, but ignore\0Asome aspects of reality, which can lead to subtle bugs. Timezone-aware\0A`datetime` objects are preferred, as they represent a specific moment in\0Atime, unlike \22naive\22 objects.\0A\0A`datetime.datetime.today()` creates a \22naive\22 object; instead, use\0A`datetime.datetime.now(tz=...)` to create a timezone-aware object.\0A\0A## Example\0A```python\0Aimport datetime\0A\0Adatetime.datetime.today()\0A```\0A\0AUse instead:\0A```python\0Aimport datetime\0A\0Adatetime.datetime.now(tz=datetime.timezone.utc)\0A```\0A\0AOr, for Python 3.11 and later:\0A```python\0Aimport datetime\0A\0Adatetime.datetime.now(tz=datetime.UTC)\0A```\0A", align 1
